🌟 Kubernetes – Simple Notes
🚀 1. What is Kubernetes? (Intro)
Kubernetes (K8s) is an open‑source container orchestration platform that automatically manages:
Deployment
Scaling
Load balancing
Self-healing
Updates of containerized applications

Created by Google, now maintained by CNCF.
👉 Think of Kubernetes as the “operating system” for containerized apps running in a cluster.

❓ 2. Why Kubernetes? (Why do we use it?)
Kubernetes solves real problems in modern cloud apps:
Scalability
Automatically increases/decreases number of containers based on load.

High Availability
Restarts failed containers → ensures app stays up.

Load Balancing
Distributes traffic to healthy containers.

Rolling Updates
Deploy new versions without downtime.

Resource Optimization
Efficiently uses CPU/RAM across nodes.

Portable
Runs on AWS, Azure, GCP, On‑prem, anywhere.
👉 Main Purpose: Run containers at scale automatically and reliably.

🏗️ 3. Kubernetes Architecture (Objects & Their Role)
Kubernetes has a Master (Control Plane) and Worker Nodes.
⭐ Control Plane Components (Brain of K8s)
1. APIServer
Entry point for all K8s commands (kubectl).
Validates and processes requests.

2. etcd
Key‑value database.
Stores entire cluster state.

3. Scheduler
Decides which node a pod should run on.

4. Controller Manager
Ensures the actual state matches desired state.
Manages:
ReplicaSets
Node health
Endpoints
Jobs etc.




🖥️ Worker Node Components (Where Apps Run)
1. Kubelet
Agent on each node.
Starts/stops containers.

2. Kube-Proxy
Manages networking & load balancing inside cluster.

3. Container Runtime
Runs containers.
Example: Docker, containerd, CRI‑O.


📦 4. Important Kubernetes Objects (Simple Explanation)
1. Pod
Smallest deployable unit.
One or more containers working together.

2. Deployment
Manages Pods.
Supports rolling updates and scaling.

3. ReplicaSet
Ensures a fixed number of Pod replicas are always running.

4. Service
Used for stable networking.
Types:
ClusterIP → internal communication
NodePort → expose service on each node
LoadBalancer → expose service to the internet

5. Namespace
Logical separation inside cluster.

6. ConfigMap
Stores non‑secret configuration (env vars, properties).

7. Secret
Stores sensitive data (passwords, tokens).

8. Volume / PersistentVolume / PVC
Storage system for Pods.


🎯 5. How Kubernetes Works (Simple Flow)
You submit a YAML → kubectl apply -f file.yaml
API server accepts request
Scheduler picks a node
Kubelet runs Pod on that node
Controller ensures desired state
Service exposes it for access

✔️ 6. One-Line Summary:-
Kubernetes is a powerful platform that automatically runs, manages, scales, and heals containerized applications across multiple machines.


WHAT IS A KUBERNETES CLUSTER?
A Kubernetes cluster is a group of machines (nodes) that work together to run containerized applications.

It has two parts:
1. Control Plane (Master Nodes)
   - Makes decisions about the cluster (scheduling, scaling, monitoring).

2. Worker Nodes
   - Run the actual application pods.

Cluster = Control Plane + Worker Nodes


TYPES OF KUBERNETES CLUSTERS:-

1. SELF-MANAGED KUBERNETES CLUSTER
(You install, configure, secure, and maintain everything)

Examples:
- kubeadm
- Bare-metal clusters
- Kubespray
- kops

Advantages:
- Full control over configuration
- Flexible customization
- Cost efficient on bare-metal
- Good for learning and on‑prem use cases

Disadvantages:
- Harder to set up
- You manage upgrades, security patches
- Requires strong DevOps/SRE skills

Used When:
- On-premise data centers
- Custom networking/storage needs
- Enterprises needing full control


2. CLOUD-MANAGED (MANAGED KUBERNETES) CLUSTER
(Cloud provider manages the control plane)

Examples:
- Amazon EKS
- Azure AKS
- Google GKE
- Oracle OKE
- DigitalOcean Kubernetes
- IBM Cloud Kubernetes

Advantages:
- Control plane managed by cloud (easy)
- Automatic upgrades and patches
- High availability built-in
- Integrates with cloud networking, storage, IAM

Disadvantages:
- Higher cost
- Less control over master components
- Cloud vendor lock-in

Used When:
- Production workloads
- Startups and enterprises
- When simplicity and reliability is required


SELF-MANAGED VS CLOUD-MANAGED (COMPARISON)

Setup              | Self-Managed: Manual (kubeadm/kops) | Cloud-Managed: Automatic
Control Plane      | You manage                          | Cloud provider manages
Upgrades           | Manual                               | Automatic
Cost               | Lower                                | Higher
Flexibility        | High                                 | Medium
Skill Needed       | High                                 | Low-Medium
Best Use Case      | On-prem/custom workloads             | Production/cloud-native


TYPES OF CLOUD KUBERNETES MODELS

1. Fully Managed Kubernetes
   (EKS, AKS, GKE)
   Cloud manages:
   - Control plane
   - Networking
   - Some security
   - Scaling

2. Hosted Kubernetes (Older Model)
   Examples:
   - Older GKE versions
   - Rancher managed clusters

3. Hybrid Kubernetes (On-prem + Cloud)
   Examples:
   - EKS Anywhere
   - GKE On-Prem
   - Azure Arc Kubernetes

Summary:
- Cluster = Control Plane + Worker Nodes
- Self-managed = Full control, more effort
- Cloud-managed = Easy, production-ready
- Cloud options = EKS, AKS, GKE
- Hybrid = Mix of on-prem and cloud


WHAT IS kubectl?
kubectl is the command-line tool used to interact with the Kubernetes cluster.

You use kubectl to:
- Deploy applications
- Check cluster resources
- Inspect logs
- Scale applications
- Debug issues
- Manage cluster objects (Pods, Deployments, Services, etc.)

kubectl communicates with the Kubernetes API Server.


BASIC KUBECTL COMMANDS (MOST IMPORTANT)

1. Cluster Information
----------------------
kubectl version
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes


2. List Resources
------------------
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods -o wide
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl get namespaces

List everything:
kubectl get all


3. Describe Resources (Detailed Info)
--------------------------------------
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name>
kubectl describe node <node-name>


4. Create / Apply YAML
------------------------
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml>
kubectl create -f <file.yaml>

Dry run (no changes made):
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml> --dry-run=client


5. Delete Resources
--------------------
kubectl delete pod <pod-name>
kubectl delete deployment <deployment-name>
kubectl delete -f <file.yaml>


6. Logs and Debugging
----------------------
View logs:
kubectl logs <pod-name>

Logs for a specific container:
kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>

Exec into pod (like SSH):
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- bash


7. Scale and Update
----------------------
Scale deployment:
kubectl scale deployment <name> --replicas=3

Update image in deployment:
kubectl set image deployment/<name> <container-name>=<new-image>


8. Namespaces
---------------
kubectl get ns
kubectl create ns <name>

Run command in specific namespace:
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Change default namespace:
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=<ns>


9. Config and Context
-----------------------
kubectl config view
kubectl config get-contexts
kubectl config use-context <context-name>


10. Apply Imperative Commands (Shortcut)
-----------------------------------------
Run a pod quickly:
kubectl run mypod --image=nginx

Expose pod as service:
kubectl expose pod mypod --port=80 --type=NodePort

KUBERNETES: IMPERATIVE VS DECLARATIVE APPROACH (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT IS IMPERATIVE APPROACH?
Imperative approach means:
“You tell Kubernetes EXACTLY what to do, step by step.”

You run kubectl commands directly to create/update/delete resources.

Examples:
- kubectl run
- kubectl create
- kubectl delete
- kubectl expose
- kubectl scale

FEATURES OF IMPERATIVE:
- Quick and easy
- Good for learning and testing
- No YAML needed
- Not ideal for production (no version control)

COMMON IMPERATIVE COMMANDS:
----------------------------------
Create a pod:
kubectl run mypod --image=nginx

Create a deployment:
kubectl create deployment mydep --image=nginx

Expose a deployment as a service:
kubectl expose deployment mydep --port=80 --type=NodePort

Scale a deployment:
kubectl scale deployment mydep --replicas=3

Delete resources:
kubectl delete pod mypod
kubectl delete deployment mydep


WHAT IS DECLARATIVE APPROACH?
Declarative approach means:
“You define the desired state in a YAML file, and Kubernetes ensures the cluster matches it.”

You use:
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml>

FEATURES OF DECLARATIVE:
- Best for production
- YAML stored in Git (GitOps)
- Version-controlled
- Reproducible deployments
- Kubernetes adjusts automatically to match the desired state

EXAMPLE DECLARATIVE YAML:
----------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mydep
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx

Apply YAML:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Update YAML and re-apply:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Delete using YAML:
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml


IMPERATIVE VS DECLARATIVE (SUMMARY)

Imperative:
- Direct commands
- No YAML required
- Good for quick tasks
- Not suitable for large/production setups

Declarative:
- Uses YAML files
- Version-controlled deployments
- Best for production
- Kubernetes maintains desired state


WHEN TO USE WHAT?

Use Imperative When:
- Testing
- Learning
- Creating something quick
- Temporary resources

Use Declarative When:
- Production applications
- CI/CD pipelines
- GitOps workflows
- Teams working together
- Anything that must be tracked and versioned

KUBERNETES LABELS AND SELECTORS (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT ARE LABELS?
Labels are key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects such as:
- Pods
- Deployments
- Services
- Nodes

Example:
  labels:
    app: frontend
    env: dev

Purpose:
- Organize resources
- Filter and group objects
- Select specific objects using selectors
- Enable rolling updates, autoscaling, and service discovery


LABEL SYNTAX:
- Key: must match DNS label format (e.g., app, env, tier)
- Value: free-form string

Examples:
app: myapp
env: prod
tier: backend


BASIC LABEL COMMANDS

Add a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> app=frontend

Update/overwrite a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> env=production --overwrite

Delete a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> env-

View labels:
kubectl get pods --show-labels

Filter using label:
kubectl get pods -l app=frontend


WHAT ARE SELECTORS?
Selectors are used to filter Kubernetes objects based on labels.

Two types:
1. Equality-based selectors:
   - app=frontend
   - env!=prod

2. Set-based selectors:
   - env in (dev,qa)
   - tier notin (cache)
   - app in (frontend,backend)


SELECTOR EXAMPLES:

Get pods with specific label:
kubectl get pods -l app=frontend

Select pods NOT having a label:
kubectl get pods -l 'env!=prod'

Select using set-based:
kubectl get pods -l 'env in (dev,qa)'
kubectl get pods -l 'tier notin (cache)'


LABELS IN YAML (EXAMPLE):

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
  labels:
    app: frontend
    env: dev
spec:
  containers:
    - name: c1
      image: nginx


WHERE SELECTORS ARE USED?

1. Deployment:
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend

2. Service:
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend

3. ReplicaSet:
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      tier: backend

Services use selectors to know:
- Which pods to send traffic to

Deployments use selectors to know:
- Which pods they control


IMPORTANT SELECTOR COMMANDS:

Get pods using multiple labels:
kubectl get pods -l 'app=frontend,env=dev'

Get deployments using selector:
kubectl get deployment -l app=frontend

Delete pods using label:
kubectl delete pod -l app=frontend

Delete any resource by label:
kubectl delete all -l app=myapp


SUMMARY:
- Labels = key-value tags on objects.
- Selectors = filters to find objects.
- Services and Deployments depend on labels/selectors.
- Set-based selectors allow advanced filtering.

KUBERNETES NODE SELECTOR (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT IS A NODE SELECTOR?
A nodeSelector is the simplest way to schedule a Pod onto specific Nodes.
It tells Kubernetes:
“Run this Pod ONLY on nodes that have this label.”

NodeSelector uses key-value pairs of node labels.

Example:
nodeSelector:
  disktype: ssd

This means: schedule the Pod only on nodes where the node label is disktype=ssd.


HOW TO LABEL A NODE:
--------------------------------
View nodes:
kubectl get nodes

Add a label:
kubectl label node <node-name> disktype=ssd

Check node labels:
kubectl get nodes --show-labels


POD YAML WITH NODE SELECTOR:
--------------------------------
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    disktype: ssd
  containers:
    - name: c1
      image: nginx

This Pod will only run on nodes labeled disktype=ssd.


WHAT IF NO NODE MATCHES?
--------------------------------
If no node has the label defined in nodeSelector:
- Pod will stay in Pending state
- Scheduler cannot place it anywhere


USE CASES OF NODE SELECTOR:
--------------------------------
- Schedule workloads on high-performance nodes
- Assign certain apps to SSD-based nodes
- Separate dev / prod workloads
- GPU nodes for ML workloads
- Node dedicated for logging, monitoring, etc.


LIMITATIONS:
--------------------------------
- Very basic scheduling method
- Exact match only (key=value)
- No advanced rules (use affinity for complex scheduling)

For advanced scheduling use:
- nodeAffinity
- taints & tolerations


SUMMARY:
--------------------------------
- nodeSelector binds a Pod to nodes with matching labels
- You must label nodes manually
- Simple and effective for basic scheduling

KUBERNETES SERVICES – NOTES
===========================

Kubernetes Services provide stable networking and consistent access to Pods.
Pods are temporary and their IPs change frequently, so Services give a fixed
endpoint to reach applications.

Kubernetes has 3 main Service types:
1. ClusterIP
2. NodePort
3. LoadBalancer

------------------------------------------------------------
1. CLUSTERIP SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Default Service type in Kubernetes.
• Exposes the application ONLY inside the cluster.
• Not accessible from outside the cluster.
• Used for internal communication between Pods or microservices.
• Provides a stable internal IP.

Use Cases:
– Backend services
– Database services
– Internal APIs

Why ClusterIP?
– Pods have dynamic IPs; ClusterIP gives a permanent internal endpoint.

Example:
type: ClusterIP

------------------------------------------------------------
2. NODEPORT SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Exposes the application externally using <NodeIP>:<NodePort>.
• NodePort range: 30000–32767.
• All Nodes expose the same port, making it reachable externally.
• Used for testing or small environments without a LoadBalancer.

Use Cases:
– Access app from outside without Ingress
– Development and testing

Why NodePort?
– Provides simple external access without cloud dependencies.

Example:
type: NodePort
nodePort: 30080

------------------------------------------------------------
3. LOADBALANCER SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Exposes the application to the internet with a PUBLIC IP.
• Cloud provider (AWS, Azure, GCP) creates a load balancer.
• Distributes traffic across multiple Nodes and Pods.
• Best for production-grade services.

Use Cases:
– Web applications
– Public APIs
– Production workloads

Why LoadBalancer?
– Provides external access + traffic balancing + high availability.

Example:
type: LoadBalancer

------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY / COMPARISON
------------------------------------------------------------
ClusterIP:
- Internal-only
- Default service
- For communication inside the cluster

NodePort:
- Accessible using <NodeIP>:NodePort
- Simple external access
- Good for testing environments

LoadBalancer:
- Provides public IP and cloud load balancer
- Best for production
- Distributes traffic to Nodes and Pods

------------------------------------------------------------
WHY WE NEED SERVICES IN KUBERNETES
------------------------------------------------------------
• Pods are temporary; their IPs change.
• Services give a stable, permanent network endpoint.
• Enable internal and external access.
• Provide load balancing across multiple Pods.
• Enable service discovery using DNS names instead of IP addresses.

BASIC CODE:-
---------------------------------
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: service-name
spec:
  type: ServiceType     # ClusterIP / NodePort / LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: app-label      # Match with pod labels
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80          # Service Port
      targetPort: 8080  # Pod/container port
      # nodePort: 30080 # Only for NodePort or LoadBalancer

to add state file to s3:-
export KOPS_STATE_STORE=s3://<Bucket_name>

KUBERNETES RC & RS NOTES
========================

------------------------------------------------------------
1. REPLICATIONCONTROLLER (RC)
------------------------------------------------------------
Definition:
- A legacy Kubernetes component that ensures a specified number of pod replicas 
  are running at any time.
- If any pod fails, RC creates a new one using its template.
- RC uses "selector" + "template" to manage pods.

Key Responsibilities:
- Maintain desired pod count
- Recreate pods if deleted or fail
- Ensure pod redundancy (high availability)

Important Fields:
- replicas: Number of pods required
- selector: Labels to identify which pods RC manages
- template: Pod blueprint (same as a Pod manifest)

YAML Structure:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ReplicationController
metadata:
  name: my-rc
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx

Limitations of RC:
- No rolling updates
- No rollback options
- No advanced strategies
- Old Kubernetes component (not recommended now)

MEMORY TRICK:
RC = selector + template + replicas  
(Think: RC is SIMPLE but LIMITED)

------------------------------------------------------------
2. REPLICASET (RS)
------------------------------------------------------------
Definition:
- A newer, advanced version of ReplicationController.
- Ensures a fixed number of pod replicas, but with more flexible label matching.
- Used internally by Deployments.

Key Responsibilities:
- Maintain desired pod count
- Auto-recreate failed or deleted pods
- Allow advanced selection via "matchLabels" and "matchExpressions"

Important Fields:
- replicas: Number of pods
- selector: Uses matchLabels / matchExpressions
- template: Defines the pod’s spec

YAML Structure:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: my-rs
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx

Advantages of RS over RC:
- More powerful selectors (matchLabels, matchExpressions)
- Used by Deployments for rolling updates
- Better control & stability
- Preferred by Kubernetes (newer standard)

MEMORY TRICK:
ReplicaSet = RC + advanced selector  
RS = RC on steroids

------------------------------------------------------------
3. RC vs RS (Difference Table)
------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE                   RC                   RS
------------------------------------------------------------
API Version            v1                   apps/v1
Selectors              selector             matchLabels / matchExpressions
Used By                Legacy               Deployment
Rolling Updates        No                   Through Deployment
Recommended?           No                   Yes
Template Support       Yes                  Yes
Purpose                Maintain replicas    Maintain replicas (modern way)

------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHICH ONE SHOULD YOU USE?
------------------------------------------------------------
- ReplicationController: Not recommended (legacy)
- ReplicaSet: Used internally but rarely written manually
- Deployment: BEST choice (manages RS automatically)

------------------------------------------------------------
5. SUMMARY
------------------------------------------------------------
- RC is old and simple; RS is new and powerful.
- Both maintain the number of pod replicas.
- RS uses advanced selectors and supports rolling updates (via Deployment).
- Always prefer Deployments in real projects.


KUBERNETES DEPLOYMENT – COMPLETE NOTES
======================================

------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS A DEPLOYMENT?
------------------------------------------------------------
A Deployment is a high‑level Kubernetes object that manages ReplicaSets,
which in turn manage Pods.

It is the MOST commonly used workload resource in Kubernetes.

Deployment provides:
• Rolling updates
• Rollbacks
• Self-healing
• Versioned release management
• Scaling

------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHY DO WE NEED A DEPLOYMENT?
------------------------------------------------------------
Using Pod / RC / RS alone is NOT enough for production.

Deployments solve these problems:
• RC has no rolling updates or rollback.
• RS has rolling update capability, but you must handle it manually.
• Pod alone has no self-healing and no replica support.

Deployment AUTOMATES everything:
• Create/replace ReplicaSets
• Rolling update automatically
• Rollback automatically
• Scaling made easy
• Version history maintained

------------------------------------------------------------
3. DEPLOYMENT YAML STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------
Basic structure:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mydeploy
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:                 # Pod template
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx

MEMORY TRICK:
Deployment = RS + rolling update + rollback + version control

------------------------------------------------------------
4. HOW DEPLOYMENT WORKS INTERNALLY
------------------------------------------------------------
Deployment → creates → ReplicaSet → creates → Pods

Sequence:
1. You apply a Deployment.
2. Deployment creates a **new ReplicaSet**.
3. ReplicaSet creates Pods according to template.
4. When you update the Deployment (image, env, etc.):
   - A NEW ReplicaSet is created.
   - Old ReplicaSet is scaled down.
   - New ReplicaSet is scaled up (rolling update).

Rollback:
- Deployment keeps history.
- You can rollback with: kubectl rollout undo deployment <name>

------------------------------------------------------------
5. ADVANTAGES OF DEPLOYMENT OVER RC & RS
------------------------------------------------------------

A. OVER REPLICATIONCONTROLLER (RC)
---------------------------------
1. Rolling Updates supported
2. Rollback supported
3. Version history kept
4. Automatically manages ReplicaSets
5. Zero downtime deployments
6. Better API (apps/v1)
7. Recommended by Kubernetes (RC is legacy)

RC → No rolling updates, no rollback  
Deployment → Both are automatic

B. OVER REPLICASET (RS)
------------------------
RS supports:
• Replicas
• Label selection

BUT has no:
• Rolling update logic
• Rollback
• Deployment strategies
• Version history

Deployment provides:
1. Automatic rolling update using new RS
2. Rollback to previous RS
3. Tracks all versions (revision history)
4. Scaling using single command
5. Traffic shifting strategies:
   • RollingUpdate
   • Recreate

RS is low‑level → Deployment is high‑level and recommended.

------------------------------------------------------------
6. DEPLOYMENT STRATEGIES
------------------------------------------------------------
A. RollingUpdate (default)
• Gradually replaces pods
• Zero downtime

B. Recreate
• Deletes all old pods first, then starts new ones
• Causes downtime but simple

Configured under:
spec:
  strategy:

------------------------------------------------------------
7. IMPORTANT DEPLOYMENT COMMANDS
------------------------------------------------------------
Create:
kubectl apply -f deploy.yml

View:
kubectl get deploy

Rollout status:
kubectl rollout status deploy/mydeploy

Pause update:
kubectl rollout pause deploy/mydeploy

Resume:
kubectl rollout resume deploy/mydeploy

Rollback:
kubectl rollout undo deploy/mydeploy

Scale:
kubectl scale deploy/mydeploy --replicas=5

------------------------------------------------------------
8. WHEN TO USE DEPLOYMENT?
------------------------------------------------------------
Use Deployment when:
• running stateless apps
• want rolling updates
• want rollback capability
• want to scale easily
• want self-healing & versioning

DO NOT USE Deployment for:
• Databases
• Stateful workloads
(for that use StatefulSet)

------------------------------------------------------------
9. SUMMARY (EASY MEMORY)
------------------------------------------------------------
Pod = Single container or multiple containers
RC  = Maintains replicas (old technology)
RS  = RC with better selector (still low level)
Deployment = RS + rolling update + rollback + scaling + automatic versioning

Deployment is ALWAYS used in real projects.


==============================
KUBERNETES ROLLOUT & HPA NOTES
==============================

------------------------------------------------------------
1. ROLLOUT (Deployment Rollout)
------------------------------------------------------------
• Rollout is the process of releasing a new version of your application
  using a Deployment.

• When you change the Deployment (image, replicas, labels, etc.),
  Kubernetes automatically creates a new ReplicaSet and replaces pods 
  gradually.

• Rollouts ensure ZERO DOWNTIME updates.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.1 Rollout Commands
------------------------------------------------------------
# Check current rollout status
kubectl rollout status deploy/<name>

# View rollout history (previous versions)
kubectl rollout history deploy/<name>

# View details of a specific revision
kubectl rollout history deploy/<name> --revision=<number>

# Roll back to previous version
kubectl rollout undo deploy/<name>

# Roll back to a specific version
kubectl rollout undo deploy/<name> --to-revision=<number>

# Restart rollout (forces re-creation of pods)
kubectl rollout restart deploy/<name>

------------------------------------------------------------
1.2 How Rollouts Work Internally
------------------------------------------------------------
• Every time pod template changes → a NEW ReplicaSet is created.
• Deployment scales UP the new RS and scales DOWN the old RS.
• Old RS is kept with 0 pods for easy rollback.
• Uses the RollingUpdate strategy by default:
    - maxSurge: number of extra pods that can be created temporarily
    - maxUnavailable: number of pods allowed to go down temporarily

------------------------------------------------------------
1.3 Rollout Strategies
------------------------------------------------------------
A) RollingUpdate (default)
   - Update happens gradually
   - Zero downtime
   - Safest in production

B) Recreate
   - Deletes all old pods first
   - Then creates new pods
   - Causes downtime

------------------------------------------------------------
1.4 Important Rollout Notes
------------------------------------------------------------
• Rollout happens ONLY when pod template changes.
• Updating image triggers a rollout.
• Updating replicas does NOT create a new revision.
• Using "kubectl set image" triggers a rollout.
• Rollback only works if revisionHistoryLimit > 0.

------------------------------------------------------------
2. HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)
------------------------------------------------------------
• HPA automatically increases or decreases the number of pods in a 
  Deployment/ReplicaSet based on CPU/Memory/Custom metrics.

• HPA ensures pods scale during high traffic and scale down during low usage.

------------------------------------------------------------
2.1 Requirements for HPA
------------------------------------------------------------
• Metrics Server must be installed.
• Deployment MUST have CPU requests defined.
    Example:
      resources:
        requests:
          cpu: 100m
• HPA uses: Utilization = (currentCPU / requestedCPU) * 100

------------------------------------------------------------
2.2 HPA Commands
------------------------------------------------------------
# Create HPA (new syntax)
kubectl autoscale deploy <name> --cpu=60% --min=2 --max=8

# View HPA
kubectl get hpa

# Describe HPA details
kubectl describe hpa <name>

------------------------------------------------------------
2.3 HPA YAML (autoscaling/v2)
------------------------------------------------------------
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: mydeploy-hpa
spec:
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 8
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: mydeploy
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 60

------------------------------------------------------------
2.4 How HPA Works Internally
------------------------------------------------------------
• Metrics Server collects metrics from kubelets.
• HPA checks metrics every 15 seconds.
• If CPU > target → scale UP pods.
• If CPU < target → scale DOWN pods.
• Scaling is smooth and controlled (prevents spikes).

------------------------------------------------------------
2.5 Testing HPA (CPU Stress Test)
------------------------------------------------------------
# Enter pod
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- sh

# Install stress (Debian-based images)
apt-get update
apt-get install -y stress

# Run load
stress --cpu 4 --timeout 300

OR universal CPU load:
yes > /dev/null &

------------------------------------------------------------
2.6 HPA Troubleshooting Tips
------------------------------------------------------------
• If "kubectl top pods" does NOT work:
    → Metrics Server is not working.

• If HPA doesn't scale:
    → CPU requests missing.

• If using custom tags (latest), node may not pull new image:
    → Use imagePullPolicy: Always

• If pod loads but HPA doesn't scale:
    → Make sure stress runs in background (&)

------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY
------------------------------------------------------------
ROLLOUT:
• Manages application updates.
• Creates new ReplicaSets.
• Supports rollback & rolling updates.
• Commands: rollout status, history, undo, restart.

HPA:
• Automatically scales pods based on CPU/Memory.
• Needs Metrics Server + CPU requests.
• Can be created using kubectl autoscale or YAML.
• Works by monitoring metrics and adjusting pod count.

===============================================
KUBERNETES STATEFULSET & DAEMONSET — NOTES
===============================================

------------------------------------------------
1. STATEFULSET
------------------------------------------------
• StatefulSet is used to manage **stateful applications**.
• It provides each Pod with a **stable identity**, **stable storage**, 
  **ordered deployment**, and **ordered scaling**.
• Used when each Pod MUST have a UNIQUE, PERSISTENT identity.

------------------------------------------------
1.1 Key Features of StatefulSet
------------------------------------------------
1) STABLE POD NAME  
   Pods are created with predictable names:  
     <statefulset-name>-0  
     <statefulset-name>-1  
     <statefulset-name>-2

2) STABLE STORAGE  
   Each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC).  
   Even if the pod is deleted, the storage remains attached.

3) ORDERED DEPLOYMENT  
   Pods start in sequence:  
   First pod → second pod → third pod  
   (index 0 → 1 → 2)

4) ORDERED SCALING  
   When scaling up or down, pods follow ordinal sequence.

5) ORDERED TERMINATION  
   Pod-2 stops first → Pod-1 → Pod-0  
   Guarantees consistent behavior for databases.

------------------------------------------------
1.2 When to Use StatefulSet
------------------------------------------------
• Databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL)
• Distributed systems (Kafka, Zookeeper, Cassandra, MongoDB)
• Any application requiring:
  - Stable IP,
  - Stable Hostname,
  - Stable Storage,
  - Ordered startup/shutdown.

------------------------------------------------
1.3 StatefulSet YAML (Basic Example)
------------------------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  serviceName: "myservice"
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: mycontainer
          image: nginx
          volumeMounts:
            - name: data
              mountPath: /var/data
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata:
        name: data
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        resources:
          requests:
            storage: 1Gi

------------------------------------------------
1.4 Important Notes
------------------------------------------------
• Requires a **Headless Service** (ClusterIP: None).
• Perfect for apps that cannot tolerate dynamic pod names.
• Pods DO NOT share storage; each pod has its own PVC.
• Deleting a StatefulSet does NOT delete its PVCs.

------------------------------------------------
2. DAEMONSET
------------------------------------------------
• DaemonSet ensures **ONE pod runs on EVERY node** in the cluster.
• Used for background processes and cluster-level services.

------------------------------------------------
2.1 Key Features of DaemonSet
------------------------------------------------
1) ONE POD PER NODE  
   Automatically schedules one pod per node.

2) AUTOMATIC ON NEW NODES  
   When a new worker node joins the cluster → pod is automatically created.

3) AUTO-DELETION ON NODE REMOVAL  
   When a node is removed → its DaemonSet pod is removed.

4) NO NEED FOR REPLICAS  
   DaemonSet decides pod count based on node count.

------------------------------------------------
2.2 When to Use DaemonSet
------------------------------------------------
• Logging agents (Fluentd, Logstash)
• Monitoring agents (Node Exporter, Prometheus, Datadog)
• Security agents (Falco)
• Storage daemons (Ceph)
• Networking components (Calico, Flannel)

------------------------------------------------
2.3 DaemonSet YAML (Basic Example)
------------------------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: mydaemon
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: mydaemon
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: mydaemon
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: agent
          image: nginx

------------------------------------------------
2.4 Important Notes
------------------------------------------------
• Automatically runs on EVERY node.
• No replicas field needed.
• Often used for system-level pods.
• For specific nodes, use nodeSelector / tolerations / affinity.

------------------------------------------------
3. STATEFULSET vs DAEMONSET vs DEPLOYMENT (SUMMARY)
------------------------------------------------
StatefulSet:
• Ordered, stable identity, stable storage, unique pods.
• Used for Databases, Kafka, MongoDB, etc.

DaemonSet:
• Runs one pod per node automatically.
• Used for monitoring/logging/network agents.

Deployment:
• Stateless apps, flexible scaling, rolling updates.
• Used for web apps, APIs, microservices.


KUBERNETES NAMESPACE - FULL NOTES (PLAIN TEXT)
=================================================

INTRODUCTION TO NAMESPACES
================================================= A Kubernetes Namespace is a logical partition inside a Kubernetes cluster. It is used to organize, isolate, and manage groups of resources such as pods, services, deployments, configmaps, secrets, etc.
Namespaces allow you to divide a single Kubernetes cluster into multiple virtual clusters.
Key points:

Helps in organizing resources
Provides isolation between teams/projects/environments
Supports RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)
Enables applying Resource Quotas


=================================================

WHY NAMESPACES ARE USED
================================================= Namespaces solve several real‑world problems:

Environment Separationdev, qa, staging, production
Team Isolationfrontend-team namespace
backend-team namespace
Avoid Resource Name ConflictsYou can have nginx service in dev and same name in prod
Security with RBACGive access only to team-specific namespaces
Resource ManagementApply CPU & memory limits
Set limits on number of pods
Better Multi-TenancyOne cluster for multiple teams/clients


=================================================

DEFAULT NAMESPACES IN KUBERNETES
================================================= Kubernetes automatically creates the following namespaces:

defaultIf you don’t specify a namespace, Kubernetes uses this one.
kube-systemContains core system components like kube-dns, scheduler, etc.
kube-publicContains publicly accessible info.
kube-node-leaseStores heartbeat/lease information for nodes.


=================================================

COMMON NAMESPACE COMMANDS
=================================================
List all namespaces:  kubectl get namespaces
Create a namespace:  kubectl create namespace dev
Delete a namespace:  kubectl delete namespace dev
Describe a namespace:  kubectl describe namespace dev
List pods from a namespace:  kubectl get pods -n dev
Apply deployment in a namespace:  kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml -n dev
View all resources in a namespace:  kubectl get all -n dev
Switch namespace using KUBECTL CONTEXT:  kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=dev

Kubens:
USAGE:
  kubens                    : list the namespaces in the current context
  kubens <NAME>             : change the active namespace of current context
  kubens -                  : switch to the previous namespace in this context
  kubens -c, --current      : show the current namespace
  kubens -h,--help          : show this message
  kubens -V,--version       : show version


=================================================

DEPLOYMENT YAML WITH NAMESPACE
================================================= Below is an example of Deployment using a specific namespace:
apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata:  name: nginx-deploy  namespace: dev spec:  replicas: 2  selector:    matchLabels:      app: nginx  template:    metadata:      labels:        app: nginx    spec:      containers:

name: nginx
image: nginx:latest



=================================================

RESOURCE QUOTA IN A NAMESPACE
================================================= Namespaces allow administrators to enforce limits using ResourceQuota.
Example ResourceQuota YAML:
apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata:  name: dev-quota  namespace: dev spec:  hard:    pods: "10"    requests.cpu: "4"    requests.memory: 8Gi    limits.cpu: "6"    limits.memory: 16Gi


=================================================

LIMIT RANGES IN NAMESPACE
================================================= LimitRange helps set default CPU & memory limit per pod/container.
Example LimitRange:
apiVersion: v1 kind: LimitRange metadata:  name: dev-limits  namespace: dev spec:  limits:

default:
cpu: "500m"      memory: "512Mi"    defaultRequest:      cpu: "200m"      memory: "256Mi"    type: Container



=================================================

RBAC WITH NAMESPACES
================================================= Namespaces make RBAC easier by restricting user access.
Example Role (namespace-scoped):
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata:  namespace: dev  name: dev-reader rules:

apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods", "services"]  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

RoleBinding to assign user:
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: RoleBinding metadata:  name: dev-binding  namespace: dev subjects:

kind: User
name: vamsi roleRef:  kind: Role  name: dev-reader  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io



=================================================

NETWORKPOLICY WITH NAMESPACES
================================================= Namespaces allow isolation of network traffic between workloads.
Example NetworkPolicy blocking external traffic:
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata:  name: deny-external  namespace: dev spec:  podSelector: {}  policyTypes:

Ingress
ingress: []



=================================================

WHEN TO USE NAMESPACES
================================================= Use namespaces when:

Multiple teams use same cluster
Multiple environments exist
RBAC rules are needed
Need cluster organization
Need quotas for teams/projects
Avoid namespaces when:

Small cluster
Only one application exists


KUBERNETES CONFIGMAPS & SECRETS — FULL TEXT NOTES (PLAIN, COPY-FRIENDLY)
=================================================

WHAT IS A CONFIGMAP?
================================================= A ConfigMap is a Kubernetes object used to store non-sensitive configuration data such as:

Key-value pairs
Environment variables
Configuration files
Command-line arguments
ConfigMaps help keep application configuration separate from the container image.


=================================================

WHY DO WE USE CONFIGMAPS?
=================================================

Makes apps portable (no hardcoded values)
Easy to update configuration
Same image can run across dev/test/prod
Centralized configuration management


=================================================

WAYS TO CREATE CONFIGMAPS
=================================================
-------------------------------------- A) Create from Literal Values --------------------------------------
Command:  kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key1=value1 --from-literal=key2=value2
-------------------------------------- B) Create from a File --------------------------------------
Example file: app.properties  db_host=localhost  db_port=3306
Command:  kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=app.properties
-------------------------------------- C) Create from a Directory --------------------------------------
Command:  kubectl create configmap config-dir --from-file=/path/to/config/dir
-------------------------------------- D) Create Using YAML --------------------------------------
apiVersion: v1 kind: ConfigMap metadata:  name: myconfig  namespace: dev data:  key1: value1  key2: value2
Apply:  kubectl apply -f myconfig.yaml


=================================================

USING CONFIGMAPS IN DEPLOYMENTS
=================================================
-------------------------------------- A) Use as Environment Variables --------------------------------------
containers:

name: app
image: nginx  env:

name: DB_HOST
valueFrom:      configMapKeyRef:        name: myconfig        key: db_host

-------------------------------------- B) Load All Keys as Env Vars --------------------------------------
containers:

name: app
image: nginx  envFrom:

configMapRef:
name: myconfig

-------------------------------------- C) Mount ConfigMap as Files --------------------------------------
volumes:

name: config-vol
configMap:    name: myconfig

containers:

name: app
image: nginx  volumeMounts:

name: config-vol
mountPath: /etc/config



=================================================

WHAT IS A SECRET?
================================================= A Secret in Kubernetes stores sensitive data like:

Passwords
API tokens
SSH keys
TLS certificates
Secrets are base64 encoded (NOT encrypted by default).


=================================================

WHY DO WE USE SECRETS?
=================================================

Prevent storing passwords in Pod specs
Restrict access via RBAC
Keys can be rotated easily
More secure than ConfigMaps


=================================================

WAYS TO CREATE SECRETS
=================================================
-------------------------------------- A) Create from Literal Values --------------------------------------
Command:  kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=username=admin --from-literal=password=Pass123
-------------------------------------- B) Create from a File --------------------------------------
Command:  kubectl create secret generic ssh-key --from-file=id_rsa
-------------------------------------- C) Create Using YAML --------------------------------------
Values must be base64 encoded. Encode using:  echo -n "admin" | base64
YAML: apiVersion: v1 kind: Secret metadata:  name: mysecret  namespace: dev type: Opaque data:  username: YWRtaW4=  password: UGFzczEyMw==
Apply:  kubectl apply -f mysecret.yaml


=================================================

USING SECRETS IN DEPLOYMENTS
=================================================
-------------------------------------- A) Use as Environment Variables --------------------------------------
containers:

name: app
image: nginx  env:

name: USERNAME
valueFrom:      secretKeyRef:        name: mysecret        key: username

-------------------------------------- B) Load All Keys as Env Vars --------------------------------------
containers:

name: app
image: nginx  envFrom:

secretRef:
name: mysecret

-------------------------------------- C) Mount Secret as Files --------------------------------------
volumes:

name: secret-vol
secret:    secretName: mysecret

containers:

name: app
image: nginx  volumeMounts:

name: secret-vol
mountPath: /etc/secret



=================================================

VIEWING CONFIGMAPS & SECRETS
=================================================
Show all ConfigMaps:  kubectl get configmaps
Show one ConfigMap:  kubectl describe configmap myconfig
Show all Secrets:  kubectl get secrets
Show one Secret:  kubectl describe secret mysecret
Decode Secret Value:  echo "BASE64_VALUE" | base64 --decode


=================================================

WHEN TO USE WHAT?
================================================= Use ConfigMap when storing:

Non-sensitive configuration
Property files
Environment variables
Hostnames, URLs, ports
Use Secret when storing:

Passwords
Tokens
SSH keys
Certificates



Kubernetes RBAC — Plain & Simple Notes
Below is a very simple plain‑text style explanation of RBAC and a simple workflow. No extra styling, no heavy formatting — just clear, easy notes.


What is RBAC in Kubernetes?
RBAC means Role‑Based Access Control. It controls who can do what inside the Kubernetes cluster. In simple words: it is permission control.


Why RBAC?

To restrict access
To give only required permissions
To protect the cluster
To follow least‑privilege rules


RBAC Has 4 Main Parts:

RoleDefines permissions in a namespace
Example: allow user to read pods in "dev" namespace
ClusterRoleSame as Role but cluster-wide
Needed for non‑namespaced resources (nodes, PVs, etc.)
RoleBindingConnects Role to a User/Group/ServiceAccount
Works only in one namespace
ClusterRoleBindingConnects ClusterRole to User/Group/ServiceAccount
Works across entire cluster


Simple Example of Role (read pods in dev)
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: dev
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]


Simple RoleBinding Example
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: read-pods-binding
  namespace: dev
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: vamsi
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io




How Kubernetes Users Are Created (Simple Explanation)
Kubernetes does NOT create users inside the cluster. We manually create:

A private key
A certificate for that user
Add the user to kubeconfig
Create a context for that user


Steps to Create a User (Simple Notes)
Step 1: Create private key
openssl genrsa -out vamsi.key 2048


Step 2: Create CSR
openssl req -new -key vamsi.key -out vamsi.csr -subj "/CN=vamsi/O=dev-team"


(CN = username, O = group)
Step 3: Sign the CSR using Kubernetes CA
openssl x509 -req -in vamsi.csr \
  -CA /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  -CAkey /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.key \
  -CAcreateserial -out vamsi.crt -days 365


Step 4: Add the user to kubeconfig
kubectl config set-credentials vamsi \
  --client-certificate=vamsi.crt \
  --client-key=vamsi.key


Step 5: Create context
kubectl config set-context vamsi-context \
  --cluster=kubernetes \
  --namespace=dev \
  --user=vamsi


Switch to user context
kubectl config use-context vamsi-context




Simple RBAC Workflow (Very Easy Version)

Create a user (key + certificate)
Add the user into kubeconfig
Create a Role (permissions)
Create a RoleBinding (assign user)
User switches context
User gets only the permissions defined in Role

RESOURCE QUOTA — SIMPLE, DETAILED NOTES (PLAIN TEXT)
ResourceQuota is a Kubernetes object used to limit how much resources a namespace can use.
ResourceQuota helps you prevent:

one team using all cluster resources
one namespace consuming all CPU or memory
uncontrolled PVC creation
unlimited pods or services
misuse of cluster resources

ResourceQuota always works per namespace.
It cannot be applied cluster-wide.

WHY RESOURCE QUOTA IS USED

To control resource consumption per team/project
To prevent abuse or misconfiguration
To ensure fair resource sharing
To enforce hard limits on CPU, memory, pods, PVCs, services
To avoid one namespace crashing the entire cluster


HOW RESOURCEQUOTA WORKS

You create a ResourceQuota object inside a namespace.
Kubernetes checks every new object (pod, pvc, service, etc.).
If the namespace is exceeding the allowed quota → Kubernetes rejects creation.

ResourceQuota + LimitRange usually work together:

ResourceQuota = total allowed resources in namespace
LimitRange = per pod/per container min-max limits


TYPES OF RESOURCES YOU CAN LIMIT
You can restrict:


Compute resources

cpu
memory
requests.cpu
requests.memory
limits.cpu
limits.memory



Object counts

pods
services
configmaps
secrets
persistentvolumeclaims (PVCs)



Storage

requests.storage
storageclass.storage.k8s.io/… (per storage class)



Hugepages (if enabled)


GPU resources (if available)



SIMPLE EXAMPLE OF RESOURCE QUOTA
Example: limit namespace to

2 CPU cores
4 GB memory
maximum 10 pods

YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: dev-quota
  namespace: dev
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "2"
    requests.memory: "4Gi"
    limits.cpu: "2"
    limits.memory: "4Gi"
    pods: "10"

Explanation:

total CPU maximum = 2 cores
total memory maximum = 4Gi
only 10 pods allowed


EXAMPLE: LIMIT OBJECT COUNT
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: object-quota
  namespace: dev
spec:
  hard:
    services: "5"
    configmaps: "10"
    secrets: "20"
    persistentvolumeclaims: "4"

This restricts how many objects the namespace can create.

EXAMPLE: STORAGE QUOTA
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: storage-quota
  namespace: dev
spec:
  hard:
    requests.storage: 20Gi
    standard.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: 10Gi

Meaning:

Total PVC storage allowed = 20Gi
In storage class "standard", max = 10Gi


HOW TO APPLY A RESOURCE QUOTA
kubectl apply -f quota.yaml

To see all quotas in a namespace:
kubectl get resourcequota -n dev

To describe and see usage:
kubectl describe resourcequota dev-quota -n dev


HOW RESOURCE QUOTA BLOCKS POD CREATION
If a developer tries to create a pod requesting 4 CPU,
but quota only allows 2 CPU in namespace:
Kubernetes will reject with error:
pods "mypod" is forbidden:
exceeds quota: dev-quota,
requested: cpu=4, used: cpu=1, limited: cpu=2


RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN RESOURCE QUOTA AND LIMITRANGE
ResourceQuota = namespace-level total resources
LimitRange = per-container or per-pod limits
Example:

ResourceQuota says: namespace max memory = 4Gi
LimitRange says: each pod can use max 1Gi

Both work together to enforce balanced use.

COMMON USE CASES

Team environments where each team has its own namespace
Multi-tenant clusters
Ensuring small namespaces cannot create massive pods
Controlling PVC storage usage
Enforcing fair use on shared clusters
Preventing accidental “OOM” of cluster due to bad deployments


HOW TO CHECK CURRENT USAGE
kubectl describe quota -n dev

Output shows:

Hard limits (max allowed)
Used limits (current usage)
Remaining resources


IMPORTANT NOTES

ResourceQuota applies at namespace level only
ResourceQuota does not directly throttle pods — it only limits creation
ResourceQuota requires requests and limits to be set on pods
If a pod has no CPU/memory request, Kubernetes assigns default (limitrange)
Without LimitRange, quota may force you to explicitly specify CPU/memory in every pod


VERY SIMPLE SUMMARY (FOR EASY REMEMBERING)
ResourceQuota = “How much can this namespace use?”
LimitRange = “How much can each pod/container use?”
RoleBinding = “What permissions does user have?”
ServiceAccount = “User inside namespace”

KUBERNETES VOLUMES — DETAILED PLAIN TEXT NOTES
In Kubernetes, containers are temporary.
If a container stops, crashes, or is recreated, all the data inside it is lost.
Since many applications need data that survives restarts (logs, uploads, database files), Kubernetes provides Volumes.
A Volume in Kubernetes is a storage directory available to containers inside a Pod.
Unlike container file systems, a Volume lives longer than individual containers.

WHAT ARE KUBERNETES VOLUMES?
A Kubernetes Volume is a storage space attached to a Pod that remains available even if containers inside the pod restart.
Volumes allow containers to:

Share files between each other
Store data temporarily or permanently
Survive container restarts
Use external storage like cloud disks

Volumes are defined at the Pod level, not at the container level.
Multiple containers inside a Pod can mount the same volume.

TYPES OF VOLUMES IN KUBERNETES
Kubernetes supports many volume types, but the key ones are:

EmptyDir
HostPath
Persistent Volume (PV)
Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)
Cloud volumes like AWS EBS, Azure Disk, GCP PD via PV

Let’s go through each one in detail.


EMPTYDIR


EmptyDir is the simplest temporary storage volume.
Important behavior:

It is created when the Pod starts running.
It is deleted when the Pod is deleted.
It survives container restarts inside the same Pod.
It is stored on the node's temporary storage (RAM or disk).
Data is NOT persistent between Pod recreation.

Common uses:

Temporary files
Caching
Intermediate computation results
Sharing data between containers in the same Pod

Data sharing:
If two containers mount the same EmptyDir volume, whatever one writes is immediately visible to the other.
EmptyDir volume example:

Container 1 writes to /tmp/shared
Container 2 sees the same file at /tmp/shared

Pod deletion = EmptyDir deleted.


HOSTPATH


HostPath mounts a directory from the host node’s filesystem into the Pod.
For example:
Host machine path:  /tmp/mydata
Pod mount path:     /app/data
HostPath behavior:

Data survives Pod deletion.
Data remains on the node as long as the node exists.
If Pod restarts on the same node → data is still present.
If Pod moves to another node → data is NOT available.
If the node dies → data is lost.

HostPath is mainly for:

Local testing
Storing logs on node filesystem
Simple single‑node clusters

Problem:
If the pod runs on Node A, data is stored on Node A.
If the pod restarts on Node B, data will not be there.
So HostPath is not good for production or multi-node clusters.


PERSISTENT VOLUME (PV)


A Persistent Volume is storage inside the cluster that is independent of Pods.
It is similar to a disk available for long-term storage.
Key points:

Created by administrator or dynamically created via StorageClass.
Has a lifecycle independent of Pods.
Stays available even if Pods delete.
Can be backed by cloud storage like:

AWS EBS
GCP PD
Azure Disk
NFS, Ceph, Cinder, etc.



PV defines:

Storage size
Access modes
Reclaim policy
Storage backend (EBS, NFS, etc.)

Pod does not directly use PV.
Instead, Pods use PVC (Persistent Volume Claim).


PERSISTENT VOLUME CLAIM (PVC)


A PVC is a request for storage by a user (developer).
PVC describes:

Required size (example: 1Gi)
Access mode (RWO, ROX, RWX)
Storage class

Kubernetes finds a PV that:

Has equal or more storage
Matches access mode
Matches storage class

If match found → PVC is bound.
If no match → PVC remains pending.
Once PVC is bound, Pods can mount it as a volume.
Pod can restart or move to another node, and data will still remain.

PV + PVC WORKFLOW

Admin creates PV
User creates PVC
Kubernetes binds PVC → PV
Pod mounts PVC
Pod reads/writes data
Pod deleted? Data remains in PV
New Pod using same PVC gets old data


AWS EBS VOLUME FACTS
Since you use Kops on AWS, this is important.
AWS EBS limitations:

EBS volume must be in the same Availability Zone as the Node.
EBS volume can attach to only one EC2 instance at a time.
Good for databases, logs, persistent workloads.
Data survives:

Pod deletion
Node restart
Worker recreation


Data does NOT survive:

EBS deletion
AZ failure (unless snapshots used)



EBS = ReadWriteOnce
Meaning:
Only one Pod on one node can mount the volume.

PV EXAMPLE
PersistentVolume example that uses AWS EBS:
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
name: pv-1
spec:
storageClassName: manual
capacity:
storage: 2Gi
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce
persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Recycle
awsElasticBlockStore:
volumeID: vol-0123456789abcdef
fsType: ext4

PVC EXAMPLE
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: pvc-1
spec:
storageClassName: manual
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce
resources:
requests:
storage: 1Gi

USING PVC IN A POD / DEPLOYMENT
Pod mounts PVC like this:
volumes:

name: devops
persistentVolumeClaim:
claimName: pvc-1

Containers access storage using:
volumeMounts:

mountPath: "/tmp/data"
name: devops


ACCESS MODES (VERY IMPORTANT)
These define how many Pods can access PV/PVC at once.

ReadWriteOnce (RWO)


Only one Pod can mount volume in read/write mode.
Most common (EBS supports only this).


ReadOnlyMany (ROX)


Multiple Pods can read the volume.
No writes allowed.


ReadWriteMany (RWX)


Multiple Pods can read and write at same time.
Not supported by EBS.
Requires EFS, NFS, CephFS, etc.


Execute (rare)


Pod can execute files but not read/write.


COMPARISON SUMMARY
EmptyDir:

Temporary
Lives only while Pod lives
Good for temporary files

HostPath:

Data stored on node
Lost if node dies
Pod must run on same node

PV/PVC:

Permanent
Survives Pod restart and movement
Good for production
Backed by cloud storage


CONCLUSION
Kubernetes Volumes help store data safely beyond the container lifecycle.
EmptyDir and HostPath are simple but not reliable for production.
Persistent Volumes with PVC provide strong, reliable, cluster‑wide storage.
Understanding PV, PVC, and access modes is essential for running real applications such as databases, logging systems, and persistent workloads.
